WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.386
Culture is the shared pattern of assumptions, values,
and norms that shapes how people work.

2
00:00:05.586 --> 00:00:07.331
It is more than a mission statement.

3
00:00:07.531 --> 00:00:09.216
Schein describes three levels.

4
00:00:09.416 --> 00:00:12.727
Artifacts are visible features, such as a meeting
routine.

5
00:00:12.927 --> 00:00:16.861
Espoused values are the principles an organization says
it follows.

6
00:00:17.061 --> 00:00:20.684
Shared basic assumptions are deeper, often unquestioned
beliefs.

7
00:00:20.884 --> 00:00:27.464
Imagine that Cedar Crossing says collaboration matters,
but its meetings recognize only individual departments'
results.

8
00:00:27.664 --> 00:00:29.820
The meeting routine is an artifact.

9
00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:32.250
Collaboration is an espoused value.

10
00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:37.156
Perhaps staff believe helping another department will
not count toward their own success.

11
00:00:37.356 --> 00:00:42.136
That last statement is a hypothesis to investigate, not
something one meeting proves.

12
00:00:42.336 --> 00:00:46.161
Leaders, history, and shared experiences all influence
culture.

13
00:00:46.361 --> 00:00:51.162
A strong culture can support cooperation or reinforce an
unhelpful habit.

14
00:00:51.362 --> 00:00:54.315
Posting a new slogan does not settle that question.

15
00:00:54.515 --> 00:01:00.584
Ask how everyday practices affect whether people
exchange information, ask for help, and raise concerns.

